1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:02,991
Forty trillion dollars.

2
00:00:05,191 --> 00:00:08,801
That's how much the United States
government owes right now.

3
00:00:09,101 --> 00:00:12,331
More than $40 trillion,
and still climbing.

4
00:00:13,131 --> 00:00:19,793
In August 2026, America's debt crossed
$40 trillion for the first time in history.

5
00:00:20,093 --> 00:00:24,199
Just five months earlier,
it was $39 trillion.

6
00:00:24,999 --> 00:00:27,740
And here's the part most
people don't realise.

7
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:35,165
In the fiscal year that just ended, America
spent $1.14 trillion on interest alone.

8
00:00:35,465 --> 00:00:37,933
That's more than its entire military.

9
00:00:38,233 --> 00:00:39,621
More than Medicare.

10
00:00:39,921 --> 00:00:42,250
Only Social Security costs more.

11
00:00:43,050 --> 00:00:44,733
Every single second,

12
00:00:44,733 --> 00:00:50,709
around $36,000 of American tax money
disappears into interest payments.

13
00:00:51,009 --> 00:00:52,369
Not into roads.

14
00:00:52,669 --> 00:00:53,932
Not into schools.

15
00:00:54,232 --> 00:00:55,596
Just interest.

16
00:00:57,796 --> 00:00:59,804
So is this the beginning of the end?

17
00:01:00,104 --> 00:01:03,311
Or is it one of the most
overhyped fears in finance?

18
00:01:03,931 --> 00:01:07,889
Stay with me, because the answer
is more surprising than you think.

19
00:01:18,089 --> 00:01:19,336
Let's start simple.

20
00:01:19,956 --> 00:01:25,756
Every year, the US government spends money
on Social Security, Medicare, defence,

21
00:01:25,756 --> 00:01:29,837
roads, schools, and interest
on the debt it already owes.

22
00:01:30,637 --> 00:01:39,631
In fiscal year 2026, it spent $7.4 trillion.
But it collected only $5.4 trillion.

23
00:01:40,431 --> 00:01:41,501
Think about that.

24
00:01:41,801 --> 00:01:48,169
For every $100 the government spent,
it brought in only about $73.

25
00:01:48,969 --> 00:01:50,386
So it borrows the rest.

26
00:01:50,686 --> 00:01:53,275
Nearly $2 trillion in a single year.

27
00:01:53,575 --> 00:01:57,517
That's about $5.5 billion,
every single day.

28
00:01:58,317 --> 00:02:02,715
It borrows by selling Treasury
bonds to investors around the world,

29
00:02:02,715 --> 00:02:05,538
and those investors
lend America the money.

30
00:02:06,158 --> 00:02:09,461
Sounds fine, right?
Countries borrow all the time.

31
00:02:10,261 --> 00:02:12,322
But here's where it turns dangerous.

32
00:02:12,942 --> 00:02:14,978
That new borrowing adds to the debt.

33
00:02:15,278 --> 00:02:17,031
More debt means more interest.

34
00:02:17,331 --> 00:02:23,072
In just one year, America's interest
bill jumped by $115 billion.

35
00:02:23,372 --> 00:02:26,508
More interest means even
more borrowing next year.

36
00:02:26,808 --> 00:02:30,240
And when old bonds come due,
America doesn't pay them off.

37
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:32,403
It borrows again to repay them.

38
00:02:33,203 --> 00:02:33,570
Today,

39
00:02:33,570 --> 00:02:38,461
more than one out of every five dollars
the government collects goes straight to

40
00:02:38,461 --> 00:02:39,011
interest.

41
00:02:39,811 --> 00:02:45,831
Split evenly, the debt now works out
to more than $115,000 for every man,

42
00:02:45,831 --> 00:02:48,057
woman and child in America.

43
00:02:48,857 --> 00:02:50,743
It's a machine that feeds itself.

44
00:02:51,043 --> 00:02:54,080
And the faster it spins,
the harder it is to stop.

45
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:04,336
Now, you might think, "Come on. It's America.
Nobody seriously doubts America."

46
00:03:04,956 --> 00:03:06,705
Except... they already have.

47
00:03:07,505 --> 00:03:08,670
In August 2011,

48
00:03:08,670 --> 00:03:15,115
a political fight over the debt ceiling
pushed the US so close to the edge that S&P

49
00:03:15,115 --> 00:03:20,861
stripped America of its perfect AAA
credit rating for the first time ever.

50
00:03:21,481 --> 00:03:25,090
In August 2023, Fitch downgraded it too.

51
00:03:25,710 --> 00:03:27,579
And in May 2025, Moody's,

52
00:03:27,579 --> 00:03:32,290
which had given America its top
rating for more than a century,

53
00:03:32,290 --> 00:03:34,010
finally cut it as well.

54
00:03:36,210 --> 00:03:38,850
For the first time in modern history,

55
00:03:38,850 --> 00:03:44,773
not one of the three major rating agencies
gives the United States a perfect score.

56
00:03:45,573 --> 00:03:47,298
And the bond market noticed.

57
00:03:47,918 --> 00:03:55,984
In August 2026, the interest rate
on America's 30-year bond climbed to 5.31%.

58
00:03:56,284 --> 00:04:02,085
That's the highest since 2007,
on the eve of the global financial crisis.

59
00:04:02,385 --> 00:04:08,930
Just days earlier, the Treasury had
to offer 5.22% to sell new 30-year bonds,

60
00:04:08,930 --> 00:04:13,010
the highest at one of those
auctions since 2001.

61
00:04:13,810 --> 00:04:18,531
In other words, the world is still lending
to America. But it's charging more.

62
00:04:19,151 --> 00:04:22,010
That doesn't mean collapse
is coming tomorrow.

63
00:04:22,010 --> 00:04:26,671
But it does mean the people whose job
is to judge risk are getting nervous.

64
00:04:31,371 --> 00:04:33,925
So how would a real crisis actually start?

65
00:04:34,225 --> 00:04:35,567
There are three doors.

66
00:04:35,867 --> 00:04:37,534
And none of them are pleasant.

67
00:04:38,584 --> 00:04:41,539
Door number one: the investors walk away.

68
00:04:42,939 --> 00:04:48,072
Imagine one morning, global investors
simply say: "We'll still lend you money...

69
00:04:48,072 --> 00:04:50,639
but only at much higher interest rates."

70
00:04:51,439 --> 00:04:52,904
Bond prices crash.

71
00:04:53,204 --> 00:04:54,284
Yields shoot up.

72
00:04:54,584 --> 00:04:58,101
Suddenly every dollar
America borrows costs more,

73
00:04:58,101 --> 00:05:01,905
every dollar it refinances
costs more, and mortgages,

74
00:05:01,905 --> 00:05:06,212
car loans and business loans
all get more expensive with it.

75
00:05:07,012 --> 00:05:10,971
And remember, interest is already
America's second-biggest bill.

76
00:05:11,271 --> 00:05:18,108
The Congressional Budget Office projects
it will reach $2.1 trillion a year by 2036,

77
00:05:18,108 --> 00:05:19,817
even without a panic.

78
00:05:20,617 --> 00:05:26,294
Think a sudden panic is far-fetched?
In 2022, it happened to the United Kingdom.

79
00:05:27,094 --> 00:05:29,060
On 23 September 2022,

80
00:05:29,060 --> 00:05:35,240
the British government announced
£45 billion of unfunded tax cuts.

81
00:05:35,540 --> 00:05:36,789
Investors panicked.

82
00:05:37,089 --> 00:05:40,768
The pound crashed to its lowest
level against the dollar in history.

83
00:05:41,068 --> 00:05:43,304
British bond markets went into freefall.

84
00:05:43,604 --> 00:05:46,925
Pension funds were pushed
to the edge of collapse,

85
00:05:46,925 --> 00:05:52,172
and the Bank of England had to pledge
up to £65 billion in an emergency rescue.

86
00:05:52,792 --> 00:05:55,993
Less than a month later,
the Prime Minister resigned.

87
00:05:58,193 --> 00:05:59,266
That was Britain.

88
00:05:59,566 --> 00:06:05,155
Now imagine it happening to the country
whose bonds the entire world depends on.

89
00:06:06,205 --> 00:06:08,972
Door number two: the dollar slowly melts.

90
00:06:10,372 --> 00:06:14,476
If the government leans on printing
money to handle its debt,

91
00:06:14,476 --> 00:06:16,561
the dollar quietly loses value.

92
00:06:16,861 --> 00:06:18,098
Prices rise.

93
00:06:18,398 --> 00:06:19,553
Savings shrink.

94
00:06:19,853 --> 00:06:23,777
And foreign investors start
asking a dangerous question:

95
00:06:23,777 --> 00:06:26,369
"Why are we holding so many dollars?"

96
00:06:27,169 --> 00:06:32,661
This one doesn't arrive with a crash.
It creeps in. And by the time people notice,

97
00:06:32,661 --> 00:06:33,867
it's already deep.

98
00:06:34,667 --> 00:06:36,775
Is it already happening? Slowly.

99
00:06:37,075 --> 00:06:38,548
Around the year 2000,

100
00:06:38,548 --> 00:06:43,880
more than 70% of the world's foreign-exchange
reserves were held in dollars.

101
00:06:44,180 --> 00:06:49,294
By mid-2026, that share
had slipped to 56.7%.

102
00:06:50,344 --> 00:06:53,696
Door number three: America
defaults... on purpose.

103
00:06:55,096 --> 00:06:56,884
This is the strangest one.

104
00:06:57,184 --> 00:07:00,018
America could have the money
and still fail to pay.

105
00:07:00,638 --> 00:07:01,250
How?

106
00:07:01,550 --> 00:07:02,513
Politics.

107
00:07:03,313 --> 00:07:06,605
By law, America can only
borrow up to a set limit.

108
00:07:06,905 --> 00:07:10,793
Right now, that limit is $41.1 trillion.

109
00:07:11,093 --> 00:07:14,952
And America is already within
about a trillion dollars of it.

110
00:07:15,252 --> 00:07:20,803
Independent estimates suggest the ceiling
could be hit sometime in 2027.

111
00:07:21,603 --> 00:07:25,393
If Congress and the White House
can't agree to raise it in time,

112
00:07:25,393 --> 00:07:26,933
payments could be delayed.

113
00:07:27,233 --> 00:07:31,000
And the moment the world's
"safest" asset misses a payment,

114
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:32,724
global markets could panic.

115
00:07:33,344 --> 00:07:35,405
Not because America is broke.

116
00:07:35,705 --> 00:07:38,101
But because America chose to stumble.

117
00:07:40,301 --> 00:07:42,135
Now, here's something important.

118
00:07:42,435 --> 00:07:44,868
A crisis is not the same as collapse.

119
00:07:45,168 --> 00:07:49,472
A country can go through a brutal
financial shock and still survive.

120
00:07:49,772 --> 00:07:55,181
But the Congressional Budget Office itself
has warned that rising debt raises the

121
00:07:55,181 --> 00:07:57,585
risk of exactly this kind of crisis,

122
00:07:57,585 --> 00:08:00,857
and leaves less room
to fight the next emergency.

123
00:08:05,557 --> 00:08:09,310
Okay. If all this is true...
why is America still standing?

124
00:08:09,930 --> 00:08:12,723
Because it holds some of the
strongest cards in the world.

125
00:08:13,523 --> 00:08:15,319
First, the dollar is still king.

126
00:08:15,619 --> 00:08:18,125
Even after that slow decline,

127
00:08:18,125 --> 00:08:25,384
the dollar makes up 56.7% of global
foreign-exchange reserves, according to the IMF.

128
00:08:25,684 --> 00:08:30,292
The euro, its closest rival,
sits at about 20.6%.

129
00:08:30,592 --> 00:08:32,100
Nothing else comes close.

130
00:08:32,900 --> 00:08:36,518
Second, America has a giant,
productive economy,

131
00:08:36,518 --> 00:08:42,096
worth more than $30 trillion a year,
with huge companies, huge innovation,

132
00:08:42,096 --> 00:08:43,830
and a massive tax base.

133
00:08:44,130 --> 00:08:49,425
Even last year, tax revenue
rose by $169 billion.

134
00:08:50,225 --> 00:08:53,101
Third, America borrows
in its own currency.

135
00:08:53,401 --> 00:08:57,449
It never has to hunt for some
foreign currency to pay its bills.

136
00:08:57,749 --> 00:09:02,065
That alone protects it from the trap
that has broken many other countries.

137
00:09:02,865 --> 00:09:06,970
And fourth, US Treasuries are
the backbone of global finance.

138
00:09:07,270 --> 00:09:11,506
Banks, governments and funds all
use them as their safest asset.

139
00:09:11,806 --> 00:09:15,548
So far, every time America
has put its bonds up for sale,

140
00:09:15,548 --> 00:09:17,845
the world has shown up to buy them.

141
00:09:18,645 --> 00:09:20,337
So America is safe, right?

142
00:09:22,357 --> 00:09:23,441
Not quite.

143
00:09:24,241 --> 00:09:28,587
Because every one of those advantages
depends on one thing: trust.

144
00:09:28,887 --> 00:09:30,980
And trust doesn't disappear slowly.

145
00:09:31,280 --> 00:09:33,255
It disappears all at once.

146
00:09:37,955 --> 00:09:43,349
People love comparing America to the
Roman Empire. And it's easy to see why.

147
00:09:44,149 --> 00:09:48,240
But a modern collapse wouldn't
look like barbarians at the gates.

148
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:50,821
It would look like a slow chain reaction.

149
00:09:51,621 --> 00:09:53,454
Deficits that never stop.

150
00:09:53,754 --> 00:10:01,987
The CBO projects the yearly deficit growing
from about $2 trillion today to $3.1

151
00:10:01,987 --> 00:10:03,736
trillion by 2036.

152
00:10:04,036 --> 00:10:05,974
Interest rates that keep climbing.

153
00:10:06,274 --> 00:10:11,851
Politicians forced into impossible
choices: raise taxes, cut benefits,

154
00:10:11,851 --> 00:10:13,445
or borrow even more.

155
00:10:13,745 --> 00:10:15,116
Then slower growth.

156
00:10:15,416 --> 00:10:16,781
Stubborn inflation.

157
00:10:17,081 --> 00:10:19,611
Investors losing faith in Treasuries.

158
00:10:20,411 --> 00:10:23,320
And here's a date to remember: 2030.

159
00:10:23,620 --> 00:10:26,329
That's when, in the CBO's projections,

160
00:10:26,329 --> 00:10:30,750
America's debt breaks the record
set right after World War II.

161
00:10:31,550 --> 00:10:33,456
And finally, the quiet part.

162
00:10:33,756 --> 00:10:37,705
America's financial superpower
status starts slipping away.

163
00:10:38,005 --> 00:10:39,081
Not in a day.

164
00:10:39,381 --> 00:10:44,644
But year by year, until one day
people realise the world has moved on.

165
00:10:46,844 --> 00:10:48,700
Is that guaranteed? No.

166
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:54,359
Smart reforms, stronger growth, or tough
decisions could still turn this around.

167
00:10:54,659 --> 00:10:59,119
But every year that nothing changes,
the window gets a little smaller.

168
00:11:03,819 --> 00:11:06,456
So, will America collapse
because of its debt?

169
00:11:07,256 --> 00:11:08,790
Here's the honest answer.

170
00:11:09,090 --> 00:11:13,628
There are three very different
futures: America going bankrupt,

171
00:11:13,628 --> 00:11:17,158
America going through
a painful financial crisis,

172
00:11:17,158 --> 00:11:22,273
or America slowly losing its crown
as the world's financial superpower.

173
00:11:22,893 --> 00:11:24,697
The first is unlikely.

174
00:11:24,997 --> 00:11:26,596
The second is possible.

175
00:11:26,896 --> 00:11:28,001
And the third?

176
00:11:28,301 --> 00:11:30,088
That's the one worth watching.

177
00:11:30,888 --> 00:11:38,475
The CBO projects the debt held by the public
rising from about 101% of the economy

178
00:11:38,475 --> 00:11:45,136
this year to 120% by 2036, higher
than at any point in American history.

179
00:11:45,436 --> 00:11:50,937
Interest costs are on track to nearly
double, to $2.1 trillion a year.

180
00:11:51,237 --> 00:11:54,508
Nobody can tell you the exact
date when things break.

181
00:11:54,808 --> 00:11:56,310
But the direction is clear.

182
00:11:57,110 --> 00:12:00,731
The real question isn't
whether America can borrow.

183
00:12:01,031 --> 00:12:04,304
It's how long the world will
keep believing it should.

184
00:12:05,404 --> 00:12:06,642
So what do you think?

185
00:12:06,942 --> 00:12:12,611
Is $40 trillion a ticking time bomb,
or a problem America will fix in time?

186
00:12:12,911 --> 00:12:14,726
Drop your answer in the comments.

187
00:12:15,346 --> 00:12:18,827
And if you want to know who
actually owns all this debt,

188
00:12:18,827 --> 00:12:23,613
or whether the dollar could lose its
throne, tell me which one you want next.

189
00:12:23,913 --> 00:12:25,935
Because that story is even scarier.
